  "body": "The specifications for WebSocket.send() https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/comms.html#dom-websocket-send doesn't seem to address the fact that while bufferedAmount is an `unsigned long`, the different types send() can take can be of size `unsigned long long`, such as Blob, or a string (depending on hardware as far as I know). It's clear that if the bufferedAmount is filled up over time (with data that is smaller than the maximum value of `unsigned long`), \"the user agent must flag the WebSocket as full and then close the WebSocket connection.\" But what if the data is larger than an `unsigned long`, which is quite possible with the types of data being used?\n\nI've discussed this with several other people and such a situation (sending such a massive amount of data at once) seems it would signal other problems, and there's no reason to not follow the same steps as if the buffer filled up normally. But I think it'd be good to have a note or similar pointing out the size difference between the types of data accepted and the maximum size of the bufferedAmount, and how to resolve that situation (if different from anything else).\n",
